-- title:
--   Transcendence of j and finiteness over K(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure, and let $\Gamma \le \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ be a subgroup of finite index containing the translation matrix $T$. Consider first the intermediate field $F_0 =$ [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101) of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$, generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the set of quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_f / \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_g$ in which $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, $f$ and $g$ are modular forms of weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, the integral power series $p_f, p_g \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ are integral $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$ in the sense of the predicate `IsIntegralQExp`, and the Laurent series attached to $p_g$ is nonzero. Its constant base change `laurentBaseChange K F₀` is the intermediate field of $K \subseteq K((q))$ generated over $K$ by the image of $F_0$ under the coefficientwise map $K((q)) \leftarrow \mathbb{Q}((q))$ induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to K$. Let $y$ be an element of this field whose underlying Laurent series is `jqModC K`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K[[q]]$ of the integral power series $E_4^3 \cdot \mathrm{dedekindEtaUnitInv}$. The conclusion is twofold: $y$ is transcendental over $K$, and `laurentBaseChange K F₀` is a finite-dimensional vector space over the subfield $K(y)$ generated by $y$. Unlike the $\mathbb{Q}$-level statement it cites, this one asserts no bound on the degree in terms of the index of $\Gamma$.
--
--   This is the standard pair of facts underlying the use of the modular function field as a finite extension of the rational function field $K(j)$: transcendence of the $q$-expansion of $j$ together with finiteness of the field of modular functions of level $\Gamma$, after constant base change to $K$, over $K(j)$. It is the hypothesis package required by the Riemann–Roch and Riemann–Hurwitz arguments on these fields, such as fibre counts, the genus of $X_1(M)$ and dimension bounds for spaces of cusp forms.
-- source:
